// topk_probe — spike: does `@materialized(storage = per_entity_topk(K =
// N))` actually work end to end (compile -> WGSL -> real GPU behavior),
// or is it type-system-only plumbing that's never been exercised?
//
// Evidence going in, all pointing the same direction:
//   - No `.sim` fixture anywhere in this repo declares `per_entity_topk`
//     storage.
//   - The only test lowering `StorageHint::PerEntityTopK` is a white-box
//     compiler unit test checking IR SHAPE, not a compiled/executed
//     kernel (`crates/dsl_compiler/src/cg/lower/view.rs`,
//     `lowers_single_fold_handler_to_view_fold_op`).
//   - That same test (and the other in-source examples of a
//     `PerEntityTopK` view) use a PLAIN `self += 1.0` scalar-accumulate
//     fold body — the exact same shape `pair_map` uses. There is no
//     "insert this scored key into my top-K list" fold-body form
//     visible anywhere (`self.slot[i] = ...`-style ring/list append).
//   - A closely-related design doc (`docs/plans/g3_threats_view_design.md`,
//     status: design-only) explicitly says the ring-append fold body
//     doesn't exist yet ("the fold body's `self += <expr>` form is
//     wrong shape... that's a multi-statement ring-append primitive,
//     not a scalar accumulate").
//
// So: does `per_entity_topk(K=4)` storage on an ordinary scalar-fold
// view even compile? And if it does, does the RUNTIME actually keep the
// top-4 highest-scoring subjects per observer, or does "K" turn out to
// be cosmetic / a no-op / silently wrong?
//
// ANSWER (crates/sims/tests — checked directly against the emitted
// WGSL, no pin needed, the shader is the proof): it compiles clean,
// zero diagnostics, and it is WRONG — worse than absent. `fold_esteem`
// (the emitted kernel) dispatches one thread per `observer_slot` and
// keeps exactly ONE `accum: f32`, indexed ONLY by that slot. `subject`
// (the view's second param) is read off the event (`local_1`) and used
// only as the fold's MATCH CONDITION, never as a storage index — so
// every `Scored` event for a given observer, regardless of subject,
// accumulates into the SAME single cell. "K" appears nowhere in the
// generated shader: no per-slot array, no insert, no compare, no
// truncate. The declared `K = 4` is silently discarded.
//
// Worse: the buffer IS allocated at full `agent_cap * agent_cap * 4`
// bytes (`runtime_core.rs`'s `view_storage_esteem_primary_buf`) — the
// SAME size as an ordinary `pair_map`, i.e. the one thing `per_entity_
// topk`'s bounded-memory promise exists for is not even honored at the
// allocation level. It costs full O(N^2) memory and computes a
// different, wrong quantity (a per-observer sum across every subject,
// not a per-observer top-K by subject) with zero warning either way.
//
// Conclusion: `per_entity_topk` is not a usable primitive today.
// Building the ring-buffer/multi-timescale memory design on top of it
// would require real engine work first (matching
// `docs/plans/g3_threats_view_design.md`'s own gap list: struct-payload
// cells, a real ring-append fold body, a per-agent-pair dispatch shape
// for view-folds, expiry) — not just a `.sim`-level declaration.

event Tick { }
event Scored { observer: AgentId, subject: AgentId, amount: f32 }

entity Watcher : Agent {
  pos: vec3,
}

@materialized(on_event = [Scored], storage = per_entity_topk(K = 4))
view esteem(observer: Agent, subject: Agent) -> f32 {
  initial: 0.0,
  on Scored { observer: o, subject: s, amount: a }
    where (o == observer) && (s == subject)
    { self += a }
  clamp: [0.0, 1000000.0],
}
